From e37c16539bdbfaf1ff4c8c67652c9f425c4f6476 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: t <tristan75015@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 02:12:37 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Kernels of the finite elements: one thread per point, strides
 of the work arrays

- Inner forces, tangent operator applied without assembly and inner forces
  of the regularization of Faltus et al.: on a GPU, one thread per
  integration point loads the data of its point in shared memory (forces
  or their increments, weight, inverse of the jacobian), then one thread
  per node sums the contributions of the points in their order. The
  accesses to the arrays of the material are coalesced in the component
  by component layout, and the results are the same to the last bit in
  all the layouts and as before. A block holds at most 128 points, 64
  elements and 1024 threads. The points of an element having more than
  128 points are processed by chunks of 128 points, in their order: the
  node threads add the contributions of a chunk to the sums of the
  previous chunks, read from and written to the global memory, so that
  the results do not depend on the number of chunks. On the host, an
  element is processed by one thread, as before.
- Work arrays: the derivatives of the unknowns and the values at the
  points of all the elements are stored in the layout of the gradients
  given to the problem (component by component if the kernels compute on
  a GPU, for the automatic layout); the deformation gradients, the vectors
  of the modified tangent operator and the pseudo-stresses of the F-bar
  formulation in the layout of the array they relate to. The kernels read
  them, and the arrays of the materials, through their strides (padding
  included): the boolean point_by_point is removed.
- IntegrationRuleOrderTest: rule of order 11 (216 points per hexahedron),
  point by point and by kernels.
